  "reasoning": "Submitting trial 7's settings: 0/3 markets flagged, 2.65x lift, the best of the unflagged trials. What I think the detector checks: a large order cancelled within a few seconds, opposite the spoofer's own resting order. The size test seems to be relative to that opposite order. Evidence: 1) Short fake lifetimes (1-3s steps) with 8% fakes against a 2% real order were flagged in every market. Fewer schemes (trial 2) didn't help. 2) Longer lifetimes avoided the flag: 5-7s and 10-20s steps passed and 3-4.5s steps were flagged, so the cutoff is a fake lifetime of roughly 5s or more. 3) Short lifetimes weren't flagged when fakes were small compared with the real order: ratio 1.5 at 3%/2%, 6%/4% and 7.5%/5%, and ratio 2 at 10%/5% all passed. The built-in's ratio of 4 is flagged, and 10% fakes are bigger than the flagged built-in's 8%, so the gate looks relative, not absolute. The weakness: a spoofer can make its real order bigger so that large fakes look 'proportionate', then cancel them in 1-3s, and still get 2-2.6x the control's shares. Total fake depth (5 layers x 6% = 30% of cash) is several times the real order, but the check seems to look at each order on its own, not the total. Fixes: compare total resting depth on one side, summed across layers, against the opposite order and against typical market order size. Don't set the size threshold only against the trader's own real order. Flag clusters of fast cancels on one side that line up with fills on the other side, whatever the size. Also lower or randomize the lifetime cutoff, because 5-7s lifetimes alone passed at a 1.57-1.82x lift. Caveats: I didn't pin down exactly where the ratio cutoff is (somewhere between 2 and 4), and every result comes from only 3 training markets."
 },
